// ============================================================
// Detection 2: Brute force followed by a successful logon (final version, V3)
// Lab: Lab 1 (AD lab: DC01 + CLIENT01)
// Table: SecurityEvent (in my free Azure Data Explorer cluster the table is named SecurityEvents - rename to match)
// Events: 4771 = Kerberos pre-authentication failed (Status 0x18 = wrong password)
//         4624 = successful logon
// Logic: one row per account. Collect failure count, first/last failure and last success,
//        then compare the times with datetime_diff.
//        datetime_diff("second", later, earlier) -> positive if the first argument is later.
//        abs(Gap) is used so that failures arriving AFTER the latest success still count
//        (threaded tools, or overlapping attack sources).
// Result on my lab data: npatel, 16 failures, Gap = -94 seconds.
// Known limits:
//   - Failure count has NO time window (16 failures here were spread over ~35 minutes).
//     In a Sentinel analytics rule, bound it with "Look up data from the last" and a
//     short "Run query every" period.
//   - 4624 includes machine-account and service logons (most of the noise in my data).
//     A production version would exclude TargetUserName ending in "$" and restrict LogonType.
// ============================================================
SecurityEvent
| where EventID in (4771, 4624)
| summarize
    Failure       = countif(EventID == 4771 and Status == "0x18"),
    First_failure = minif(TimeGenerated, EventID == 4771 and Status == "0x18"),
    Last_success  = maxif(TimeGenerated, EventID == 4624),
    Last_failure  = maxif(TimeGenerated, EventID == 4771 and Status == "0x18")
    by TargetUserName
| extend Gap = datetime_diff("second", Last_success, Last_failure)
| where Failure >= 5 and Last_success > First_failure and abs(Gap) <= 900
